{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The fast-path harness ran (CheckRun d8502f6858ca421a80fe527efafffdd8, exit 0) and witnessed what the source predicted. With the single node in Leader state at term 2 and its main loop provably inside its own restore handler, one empty heartbeat carrying term 3 was dispatched the way a fast-path transport dispatches it - synchronously to the installed handler on the caller's goroutine - and came back successful, while the node's term already read 3 and its state already read Follower at the moment of sampling inside that freeze window; both values were unchanged after release, so the write is attributable to the fast-path goroutine rather than the main loop. Since this package reads and writes term and state atomically, the samples cannot be a torn read, which is precisely why the mutation is visible from outside the main thread. I recorded the behaviour that makes this reachable (the network transport's heartbeat classification and synchronous dispatch to processHeartbeat, which calls appendEntries, both documented main-thread-only, with the in-memory transport no-opping the handler) and the observed fact with its receipt. The judgment I recorded is deliberately not a defect claim: the claim is unresolved. What is established is a witnessed violation of the package's own main-thread-only invariant on a path the network transport takes for every empty heartbeat, and the guard keeps entries and commit-index updates out of that path but not the term logic, which runs before the guard's subject matter is consulted. What is not established is harm, and I did not dress the observation up as a defect: the observed transition is one Raft tolerates and the node ends in a consistent higher term as a follower. The candidate consequences are recorded as candidates, a read-modify-write on the term in the candidate path persisting a lower term if a higher one is written off-thread in between, and a leader continuing its loop iteration after its state was cleared off-thread, and the gap I registered asks for one of them to be witnessed next. Counterweights and limits are recorded, including that the harness supplies the transport wrapper rather than exercising the real socket path and that this backend runs without the race detector. The four earlier findings remain recorded and unaffected.\"}"}}
